{"setup":"Use vertices 0,1,2,3 with edges oriented 0→1, 1→2, 1→3. Let Ω=2, b=(−1/2,1/6,1/3,0), ω=(3/2,13/6,7/3,2), couplings K=(5/6,13/15,1/2), and cut flows f=(1/2,1/3,0). These are rational input data.","calculation":"The incidence equation is Bf=(−f₀,f₀−f₁−f₂,f₁,f₂)=b. The edge sines are s=(3/5,5/13,0), and choose positive cosines c=(4/5,12/13,1). The exact unit phasors are z₀=1, z₁=(4+3i)/5, z₂=(33+56i)/65 and z₃=(4+3i)/5. Their head-tail ratios have precisely those rational real and imaginary parts. Equivalently, choose phases (0,α,α+β,α), where α=arctan(3/4) and β=arctan(5/12), before subtracting the mean. All edge differences are principal and even satisfy |sinδ_e|<4/5, a strict certificate for the cohesive bound γ=arcsin(4/5).

The Hessian weights are w=Kc=(2/3,4/5,1/2). Every principal cofactor of the stable weighted tree Laplacian equals their product 4/15, and its kernel is exactly the common-phase line. The unweighted four-node star has λ₂=1, so the transverse rate obeys 1/2≤λ₂(L_w)≤2/3, the upper bound using the leaf-3 cut. These bounds and the rational Laplacian entries can be checked without evaluating any inverse trigonometric function. Flipping any subset of the three edge cosine signs, while leaving the sine values fixed, constructs all eight phase-locked branches with rational phasors. Their unstable dimensions have counts 1,3,3,1 for indices 0,1,2,3; the stable branch is the sole index-zero case.

Now retain K and the first two flows but replace f₂ by q∈(−1/2,1/2). This changes b to (−1/2,1/6−q,1/3,q). The third weight is w₂(q)=√(1/4−q²), which is always the minimum weight. Consequently w₂(q)≤λ₂(L_w(q))≤(4/3)w₂(q), while dδ₂/dq=1/w₂(q) and the mean-zero phase sensitivity norm is √3/(2w₂(q)). Thus λ₂ times the edge-phase sensitivity stays between 1 and 4/3, while the rate vanishes and sensitivity diverges at either saturation boundary. The rational parametrization q=t/(1+t²), w₂=(1−t²)/(2(1+t²)) for 0≤t<1 supplies exact examples arbitrarily close to the boundary.","conclusion":"The nonlinear equilibrium, its positive Hessian and its cut-flow certificate are explicit. The parameter family proves a quantitative relation between critical slowing and ill-conditioned phase reconstruction. The separate exact star trajectory proves that a stable network can still be invisible to an inadequate observation panel."}
